"""The Data & help page, rewritten for rugby league."""

def apply(js, rep, resub, replace_function):
    return replace_function(js, 'helpHTML', HELP, 'help')

HELP = r"""function helpHTML(){return `${sectionHeader('Database & game rules','Inside 6 Again.')}${settingsCardHTML(game())}
<section class="card"><h2>Two leagues, one player market</h2><p class="sub">${CLUBS.length} clubs and ${DB.players.length.toLocaleString()} players: the seventeen NRL clubs and the fourteen Super League clubs of 2026, plus the international pools of fourteen Test nations. Club squads are compiled from 2026 public squad reporting and the game's best knowledge of who plays where; they are not an official register and may contain mistakes or be out of date. Where a real squad is short, fictional depth players fill it out, and the smaller Test nations are topped up with fictional pool players. Every fictional player says so on his profile.</p><p class="sub">Players appear at one club only. In-game loans (both yours and the computer's) return the player to his registered club at the next season transition. A player's nation is the one he represents, and Australians carry their State of Origin eligibility.</p>${Object.entries(LEAGUES).map(([key,l])=>`<details class="disclosure"><summary>${l.name} · ${l.teams.length} clubs</summary><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Club</th><th>Players</th></tr>${l.teams.map(c=>`<tr><td>${esc(c)}</td><td>${DB.meta.clubCounts[c]}</td></tr>`).join('')}</table></div></details>`).join('')}<p class="tiny muted hint">All ratings, form, injuries, potential, market values and contracts are fictional game estimates, not official assessments or real financial records. All finances use A$ for consistency across both leagues.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Season formats</h2>${Object.entries(LEAGUES).map(([k,l])=>`<h3>${l.name}</h3><p class="sub">${competitionDescription({league:k})}</p>`).join('')}<p class="sub">Two competition points for a win, one for a draw and, in the NRL, two for a bye. Ties on points are split by points difference, then points scored. Every match level after eighty minutes goes to <b>golden point</b>: up to ten more minutes, first score wins. In the regular season it can still end a draw; in finals and World Cup knockouts it is played to a finish.</p><p class="tiny muted hint">The draws are generated and omit real dates, magic rounds and pre-season trials. Salary caps are simplified; promotion and relegation are not simulated. Later seasons continue the same structure.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>How a match works</h2><p class="sub">Thirteen a side and four on the interchange. A match is played minute by minute: possession and field position are shared out, then each side can make line breaks, score from close range or from an attacking kick, make errors (the other side gets the scrum feed), concede six agains at the ruck, give away penalties, kick on the last tackle, tackle and tire. A try is worth 4, a conversion 2, a penalty goal 2 and a field goal 1. A <b>six again</b> is a set restart for a ruck infringement: six fresh tackles, no penalty. A penalty in range can be kicked at goal for two; otherwise the attacking side kicks for touch and taps ten metres in for a fresh set on the opposition line. A grubber forced dead earns a goal-line drop-out and another set; a ball kicked dead in-goal gives the defence a seven-tackle set from their twenty. A <b>40/20</b>, a kick from your own half that bounces into touch inside their twenty, wins the feed and a new set.</p><p class="sub">Match stats track possession, territory, completed sets, tackles and missed tackles, line breaks, errors, penalties, six agains won, forced drop-outs, offloads and kicks.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Managing players</h2><p class="sub"><b>Ratings work like a power ranking.</b> After every match each player gets a match rating from what he actually did: tackles, metres, line breaks, errors forced, ruck speed, goal kicking, tries, try assists, discipline and the result. It is judged against what his position normally delivers, so a middle forward is rated on his metres and his tackle count and a winger on metres, line breaks and tries. His rating is his ability and changes only as he develops. <b>Form</b> is separate, shown as one to five stars: each performance is judged against what is expected of a player of his standard, so a modest player can reach five stars with a run of good games while a star needs big ones, and form fades back towards three stars over the following games. Five stars plays about five points above his rating, three stars at it, two stars six below and one star twelve below. Players left out lose sharpness and regulars left on the bench lose morale. Fitness and morale affect match strength, and tired players are more likely to get hurt.</p><p class="sub"><b>Potential</b> is a player's projected peak. Up to the age of 26 it rises with minutes and good performances and falls with poor ones, with serious injuries and with a season of fewer than three first-grade appearances. Every off-season a young player closes part of the gap to his potential, fastest in his late teens and early twenties.</p><p class="sub">With <b>Auto-pick every match</b> switched on (Matchday), your assistant names the strongest available 17 before each game. Change any jersey yourself and auto-pick switches off, so your selection is kept; if someone in it is injured or banned, only that jersey is filled. If injuries and departures leave you without 17 fit players, academy players are called up so a match can always go ahead.</p><p class="sub"><b>Two positions.</b> Every player has a main position and a second position, shown together (for example HB/FE for a halfback who also plays five-eighth). At his main position a player gives his full rating; at his second he loses a little; in a neighbouring role, such as a centre on the wing or a lock in the second row, he loses more, and well out of position he loses a lot. The interchange is a utility hooker, two props and a back-rower: when a back goes down, the game reshuffles, just as it does on a Friday night.</p><p class="sub">Use the star in Transfers to shortlist a player. Compare checks the target against your strongest player at that position. Wages are charged equally across regular-season rounds. Fees and signing bonuses are paid immediately. Every contract runs to the end of a named season, and you pick the end year when you sign or renew a player. Players over 31 sign shorter deals, and an unhappy player, or a star at a smaller club, may refuse to extend. Computer clubs renew or release their own players, sign free agents and loan players to cover gaps each off-season, and occasionally enquire about your unsettled or star players mid-season.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Long careers</h2><p class="sub">Ages are estimates from birth years, marked “est.”. Players develop towards their potential until about 27, level out in their late twenties and decline from 31, faster from 33. Most players retire between 32 and 36, and free agents who stay unsigned drift out of the game. Every computer club promotes two or three fictional academy graduates each off-season, so the talent pool renews itself.</p><p class="sub">Transfer prices depend on age, potential, form and contract length. A player entering his final year is much cheaper, and a player settled at a strong club can refuse to move. Squads are capped at 36. Every club works under a salary cap: A$13.5m in the NRL and A$7.0m in Super League, rising 2.5% a season. Signings, renewals and loans must fit under it. Releasing a player costs half of what is left on his contract. Match-day income and the season grant rise with club reputation, and the board lowers your reputation if you finish a season in debt.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Careers and tournaments</h2><p class="sub"><b>Careers</b> run season after season: the NRL, Super League, the international career, the hero career and the player career. Players age, develop and retire, and a board or federation judges every season. <b>Tournaments</b> are a single competition from the first match to the final: the 2026 Rugby League World Cup and the World Club Series.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>State of Origin</h2><p class="sub">Three Origin matches split the NRL season. The New South Wales and Queensland selectors pick the best seventeen eligible players from NRL clubs, and every one of them misses the NRL round of his Origin game, at your club and every rival. Overview warns you a few rounds ahead and lists who is likely to go, players come back tired and occasionally hurt, and the series score sits on your dashboard. Depth wins premierships in Origin years.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Player career</h2><p class="sub">Create a player (name, nationality, position and background) and start at 18 on a development contract with one of three clubs that want you. You do not pick the team: the coach picks the strongest 17 he can, and his <b>trust</b> in you counts. Every week you choose how hard to train and what to work on: hard weeks make you better faster and win the coaches over, but cost freshness on match day and risk injury. Weekly events shape your trust, fame and form.</p><p class="sub">In a live match the game stops for <b>your moments</b>: a line break, the last-ditch cover tackle, a bomb coming down on you, five metres out, the ruck battle, and the moments of your position. Halves choose between the bomb, the grubber and running the last; hookers decide at dummy-half; forwards can charge down the kick; halves and fullbacks can go for the 40/20; outside backs can jam in for the intercept. If you have the tee you take <b>every kick</b>: each conversion, every penalty goal and the field goals.</p><p class="sub"><b>The captaincy</b> is earned: five seasons at a club, or three once you are 25. As captain you decide what to do with every penalty in their half (the two points, touch and a fresh set, or a tap).</p><p class="sub"><b>Test football</b> follows the club season on the Test rugby tab: mid-season Tests in June, then the Pacific Championships, the European Championship or the Ashes at the end of the year, and the World Cup every four years. Play each Test live or take a quick result. Tests count as caps, and a big year can win you the Golden Boot. You can retire from 32 and step straight into a hero coaching career in the same world.</p><p class="sub"><b>Goal kicking</b> happens on a pitch seen from above: the ball on its spot, the posts on the try line and the wind. From a wide conversion, first choose your spot: further back opens the angle but adds distance. Then set your line and choose where your boot meets the ball. The ball flies, bending with the swing, your natural pull and the wind, and you watch it go over, wide, short or off the post. <b>Kicks for touch</b> from a penalty are yours too: the closer to their line, the better the fresh set.</p><p class="sub"><b>Money, contracts and energy</b> work as in any long career: wages, sponsors and match fees go into the bank; you can hire personal staff, buy a home, and choose your contract length. When your energy drops below 65% the coach asks to rest you. Miss most of the coach's targets with seasons left on your deal and the club may release you.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Game-changing moments</h2><p class="sub">A live match stops only for the decisions that can swing it: two or three at most, and mostly when the game is close and late. Take the two or kick for touch; bomb, grubber or run the last; set up the field goal or play for the try; short kick-off or kick long; wrestle the ruck, go for the strip or get back. Some moments lead straight into the next decision. Each option shows its chance, but winning the moment does not win you the match. Turn them off on the Tactics tab to let your assistant decide.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Difficulty</h2><p class="sub">Choose <b>Easy</b>, <b>Normal</b>, <b>Hard</b> or <b>Legendary</b> when you start a career, and change it at any time on the Tactics tab. On Normal and above, rival coaches study your last five matches and often set up to stop your usual plan, so a coach who never changes his tactics is easy to read. Harder levels make rivals raise their game against you and the board less patient. In a player career, difficulty sets the odds of your moments and the speed of the kicking markers.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Hero career</h2><p class="sub">One coach, one career, across clubs and national teams. You start at 31 in one of the six weakest jobs in the game with a reputation of 8 out of 100. Every club and national team sits on one <b>coaching ladder</b>, ranked by prestige. At the end of every season your reputation moves: objectives met raise it, trophies add a lot (a World Cup most of all), and jobs up to about 12 points above your reputation may call. Get sacked and only smaller jobs will have you. The Coach tab shows the ladder, your career path, your trophy cabinet and the milestones still to tick off. You retire at 70, or sooner if you choose.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>International career</h2><p class="sub">Take charge of any of the ${NATIONS.length} Test nations. Each year has two windows, and in each one you name a squad of 24 (your last squad carries over, with anyone injured or suspended replaced by the best fit player in his position).</p><p class="sub"><b>June:</b> mid-season Tests, while the NRL stops for Origin. <b>October and November:</b> the Pacific Championships (Australia, New Zealand, Tonga and Samoa), the Pacific Bowl (Papua New Guinea, Fiji and the Cook Islands) and the European Championship (France, Wales, Ireland and Scotland); everyone else plays Tests. Every fourth year from 2025 England and Australia meet in a three-Test <b>Ashes</b> series, and Australia sit out the Pacific Championships. <b>Every four years</b> the World Cup replaces the end-of-year window: 2026 in Australia, then England in 2030 and New Zealand in 2034 (later hosts are fictional).</p><p class="sub">The federation sets an objective for every competition and Test window from your world ranking. The World Cup counts most; Test windows count least. If job security is below 15% at the end of a season you are sacked and can take a job with another nation. After a World Cup that goes well, stronger nations may approach you.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Rugby League World Cup 2026</h2><p class="sub">Ten nations in Australia, in the real 2026 format. Group A (Australia, New Zealand, Fiji and the Cook Islands) plays a round robin. Groups B (England, Samoa and Lebanon) and C (Tonga, Papua New Guinea and France) play each other, three matches each, on one combined table. The top two of Group A and the top two of the combined B and C table meet in the semi-finals, then the final. Australia, as hosts, get home advantage. Choose any nation, select 24 players from its pool and submit them; the others pick automatically. Minimum position cover is a game rule: three props, two hookers, three second-rowers, a lock, a halfback, a five-eighth, two centres and four outside backs.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>World Club Series</h2><p class="sub">A fictional global club competition, entirely optional. It pools all ${CLUBS.length} NRL and Super League clubs, seeds them by squad strength and star power, and takes the top 16 into four pools of four, then quarter-finals, semi-finals and a Grand Final at a neutral venue. Inside a club career the World ranking tab keeps a live ladder: every club carries ranking points, and after every match the winner takes points from the loser. Whenever your season ends, if your club is in the top 16, Overview offers to enter the World Club Series as a bonus campaign before you begin next season. Your domestic result is untouched.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Selecting your 17</h2><p class="sub">Quick picks keep every player in his own position unless injuries or bans leave nobody who plays there. <b>Best team</b> picks the strongest side, <b>Rotated squad</b> rests tired players and gives the fringe minutes, and <b>Prospects</b> gives young, high-potential players the jerseys. Save a <b>first-choice 17</b> to keep the players you want playing as often as possible.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Playing styles and tactics</h2><p class="sub">Every player has a playing style, such as Metre-eater, Ball-playing lock, Running dummy-half, Edge runner, Game manager, Playmaker, Finisher or Kick-return fullback. Well-known players have the style they are known for; everyone else is assessed from his profile. Each style suits one or two tactical options: the more suited players in your 17, the bigger that option's edge and the faster your squad learns it.</p><p class="sub">Your game plan has six axes: attack focus (middle, balanced or edges), kicking game (field position, balanced or ball in hand), line speed (up-and-in, balanced or slide), the ruck (wrestle, balanced or get back), attack shape (direct, balanced or wide) and set tempo (complete sets, balanced or broken play). Each option leans on one of your units and has counters. Mastery builds slowly with matches and training; options you stop using fade, and squad turnover costs some of what the group knew.</p><p class="sub">Match strength is built from eight team units, each worked out from the players in the right shirts: middle forwards, ruck speed (hooker and middle forwards), edge attack, defence, ruck control, kicking game (your best two kickers among 7, 6 and 1), yardage and kick returns (the back three) and discipline.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>The board and club finances</h2><p class="sub">In a club career the board sets a ladder target from where your squad's strength ranks, asks you to give young players starts and to stay in credit and under the salary cap. Its confidence rises and falls with each season review. Below 15% you are sacked and can take over one of the league's struggling clubs. Money is tight by design: income depends on reputation, form and home games, and a financial crisis freezes signings until the books balance again. Selling, releasing or loaning a player out is never blocked.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Match centre, mentality and team talks</h2><p class="sub">Play match opens the match centre: your win chance, a unit-by-unit comparison, both game plans and the tactical edges between them. Before kick-off and at half-time you choose a team talk. Kick off to follow the game live, minute by minute, with interchanges, sin bins, injuries and the running score; pause, change speed or skip to full time at any point.</p><p class="sub"><b>Mentality</b> is how hard you push the plan, and you change it live: Shut up shop ← Defensive ← Balanced → Attacking → All-out attack. Attacking mentalities offload, support and take the tap rather than the two, so you break the line more; they also bring errors, penalties, long-range tries against and heavy legs. Defensive mentalities complete their sets and kick for field position. Every opposition coach makes the same calls from the scoreboard, the clock and his own temperament.</p><p class="sub"><b>Weather.</b> Every ground has its own climate. Rain makes the ball slippery and errors rise, most for wide, broken-play attack. Wind blowing end to end hands field position and goal-kicking range to the side with it. Heat drains energy faster. The Melbourne roof is always closed.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Squad roles, staff, inbox and injuries</h2><p class="sub">Every player at your club has a squad role (key player, first team, rotation, prospect or backup) that promises a share of the matches; players short of what their role promises lose morale. Six backroom roles on the Staff tab add a little to the units they look after, speed up learning and development, cut injuries and trim transfer costs. The Inbox holds the situations that need your call: unhappy players, agents, the board after a losing run, the press before a derby. Every injury is a named diagnosis with a realistic lay-off, and a concussion carries a compulsory stand-down.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Contracts and future transfers</h2><p class="sub"><b>The transfer window</b> is open from the off-season until round 8. After that, deals agreed only take effect at the end of the season, and loans are closed; free agents can sign at any time. Transfer-list a player and offers arrive over the next few rounds. You can sign players for the future: <b>Next season</b>, or a <b>pre-contract</b> for a player with two seasons or fewer left. Rival clubs do the same to you. The Squad planner shows this season and the next four.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Play offline on your phone</h2><p class="sub">6 Again is a single file with no online parts, so once it is on your phone it plays anywhere. Open it in your phone's browser with a connection, then add it to your Home Screen: in Safari, tap Share then <b>Add to Home Screen</b>; in Chrome, tap ⋮ then <b>Install app</b>. It also ships as an Expo app in the repository.</p>${standaloneApp()?'<p class="tiny up">You are playing the installed app.</p>':''}</section>
<section class="card"><h2>Keep your careers</h2><p class="sub">One separate save for each league, one for each kind of career and one for each tournament. Saved careers retain every final score and trophy; detailed timelines are kept for your latest 40 matches. Export before clearing browser data or moving devices. If a saved career cannot be read, it is set aside untouched rather than deleted.</p><div class="settings-action"><button class="primary" data-act="export">Export all careers</button><button class="ghost" data-act="import">Import backup</button></div></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Built from Touchline</h2><p class="sub">6 Again is the rugby league edition of Touchline: the same careers, transfer market, staff, inbox, live match centre and player career, rebuilt around thirteen-a-side, the six-tackle set, the NRL, Super League, State of Origin and the Rugby League World Cup.</p></section>`}"""
